00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's program we hear from the  north sea oil and gas workers  forced to pay for training they can't  afford or risk losing work as the uk  moves away from fossil fuels to  renewable energy  political point scoring or a way to  protect a world heritage site  the row over classing the great barrier  reef as  in danger heats up and how your home  could become a mini power station to  help the uk  reach its climate targets  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes that are  happening to our world right now  and we meet those coming up with the  solutions well as countries around the  world attempt to reduce emissions from  fossil fuels  the energy sector needs to undergo a  huge transformation  with oil and gas making way for  renewables  the north sea transition deal agreed by  the government in march  aims to help workers in those sectors  retrain  and move into clean energy but that is  not necessarily happening  a survey of more than 600 north sea oil  and gas workers  found that workers themselves are having  to pay  nearly 2 000 pounds a year in training  costs  nearly two-thirds said that in the past  two years their employer contributed  nothing financially to their retraining  and nearly three-quarters of those  surveyed are contractors without  guaranteed employment  opportunities and 94 said that they  would support a solution in the form of  an  offshore passport which would  effectively license all  accredited workers to work offshore in  any sector  our climate correspondent lisa holland  reports now from aberdeen  when the weather's good there's a  regular flow of helicopters ferrying oil  and gas workers from aberdeen airport  to rigs across the north sea  at a nearby pub the relief of getting  back to shore  is clear but change the subject to  people's futures and finding green jobs  and nobody wants to speak openly for  fear of being blacklisted within the  industry  they are really really angry everyone is  really angry in the oil field at the  minute  this man has worked in oil and gas for  18 years  when i started it was good times but i  know a lot of people have lost their  jobs  how much help do you feel is being given  to people in the oil and gas industry to  find a new job  i haven't personally seen any help like  that and nobody i've ever spoken to in  my time offshore  has ever said they're getting help that  uncertainty is felt across aberdeen  a city built around the oil boom of the  70s  after 42 years in oil and gas norman is  close to retirement  and for that reason agreed to show his  identity  he says he never imagined such  uncertainty  i want to finish my working career  working i don't want to finish like this  this is  not good for anyone but  for a young guy and he stopped these i  would say it would be  pretty devastating in the harbour oil  and gas supply ships await instructions  wind farms at sea point to a future of  renewables  which nobody quite knows when and how  will overtake the city's past  lisa holland sky news aberdeen  well let's bring in deidre mickey now  chief executive of  oil and gas uk deirdre good to talk to  you  you represent an industry that's  effectively in  managed decline and the workers we were  hearing from there  clearly don't have any confidence that  anyone's looking out for them  yeah good afternoon sam um i think it's  a really interesting report  um that greenpeace and um friends of  their put out today  because uh you know and welcome it  because it acknowledges the role  that this sector and the workforce have  to play in delivering a successful  energy transition  and i think the thing that wasn't didn't  come out of your report was how much  change is going on in the sector you  know many of our  companies are already into all the other  energies like hydrogen  carbon capture storage and renewables as  well as oil and gas  and it's really important that they do  move into all these sectors because we  are going to need oil and gas for years  to come  and so the skills and the expertise that  we have today  in our sector will be absolutely crucial  to these  emerging energy sectors going forward  and deity how would this offshore  passport work  so so one of the things that the report  speaks to is removing the barriers so  that people can  move seamlessly between the different  sectors  and that's something you absolutely  support that principle  whether it's a passport or whether it's  it's another means of aligning  in terms of different standards and  approaches  you know that's something that we need  to work on but the principle  is absolutely right but we should be  reducing as many barriers as we can  so that people can work in oil and gas  they can move into  hydrogen they can move into offshore  wind and that they can do that  relatively seamlessly and relatively  easily  did you make it good to talk to you  thank you  thanks very much well in today's other  climate news  the uk's sustainable aviation alliance  has set  new co2 targets in a bid to reach net  zero by 2050.  airlines airports and other service  providers have promised to reduce or  indeed offset  the sector's net emissions by 15 by 2030  and by 40 in 2040. however those targets  will have to be met by offsetting  schemes and technology  which is not yet fully commercialized  smart traffic lights which tell  motorists how long is left until they  change to red  could be introduced in england to reduce  co2  emissions a trial of the new technology  found vehicle emissions were cut by more  than a quarter  as it minimised the number of  unnecessary stops  it also led to a 17 reduction in  nitrogen oxide levels from engines  which is linked to poor air quality  gabon has become the first country in  africa to be rewarded for  lowering its co2 emissions the un's  central african forest initiative has  paid the country  17 million dollars for reducing  greenhouse gas emissions  in 2016 and 2017. gabon says the money  will be invested into  low-carbon strategies which can help  reduce co2 levels and protect  biodiversity  and the duchess of cambridge has visited  the natural history museum to hear about  its urban  nature project the project aims to help  communities across the uk to understand  the importance of nature  and find practical solutions to protect  our planet's future  well during her visit the duchess joined  local school children in the museum's  wildlife garden  and took part in activities including  spider-making and  storytelling now the great barrier reef  is one of the world's natural wonders  and its coral reefs play a crucial role  in our environment  but now the un's cultural body unesco  wants it added to a list of  endangered world heritage sites due to  the impact of climate change  australia has reacted angrily to the  recommendation after it has spent  years trying to keep the reef off that  list  and its government says it will oppose  the plan  here's everything you need to know about  the row  [Music]  this decision was flawed clearly there  were politics behind it  clearly those politics have subverted  a proper process  [Music]  i will stand up in any community and i  do  and say that we have the best managed  reef in the world  [Music]  the values are definitely in danger so  from that perspective yes  the world heritage property should be  listed in danger it's not a permanent  listing  and many properties have come off the  endangered list once they can  show that they have addressed the  concerns that have been raised by the  committee  now big changes are going to be needed  to our homes if the uk  is to reach its target of net zero  emissions by 2050  according to a new report published by  good energy  well it found that in the next 30 years  13 and a half  billion homes are going to need to have  rooftop solar panels  more than 80 of heating will need to be  electrified that's rather than using  natural gas  and homes should have battery walls to  allow residents to store and  use renewable energy when they need it  and driverless electric vehicles will  need to become the norm  using technology to send power back to  the grid  in times of high demand well joining me  now is juliette davenport  founder of good energy juliet good to  talk to you  it's clear isn't it that households need  to do  their bit but even most new builds don't  have any of those elements yet  completely and it's i mean that what the  reports lays out is that we've  investigated  looking at taking renewables out to the  nation  and what else do you need and what is  clear is the housing stock in the uk  really lags behind the investments that  we've made seen in other european  countries  so it's under insulated it doesn't we  we don't really consider it as part of  our energy infrastructure  whereas actually we could turn it into a  benefit where we could see  consumers generating their own power and  being an active part of the  the the power network by sending power  back as you said from  either their electric vehicle or storage  in their own homes  julia when we look at our power mix that  percentage that comes  from renewables needs to be much much  higher  doesn't it and what kind of  infrastructure do we need in place to  support  that change so i think you touched on  earlier on offshore wind is going to be  a really important part of this mix so  making sure that we've got  an industry the skills and technology to  be able to deliver offshore wind  and the power systems to be able to to  bring that in from offshore  but we're also going to see um from from  our analysis  we see some larger scale solar going in  so again we're going to need  the capability of taking power  delivering power locally  we're going to need to see uh geothermal  so geothermal is a technology  there's not much use but actually we've  got abundant sources in the uk and we're  already beginning to see  places like the eden center explore for  geothermal  and tidal tidal is going to be a really  important technology  something that as an island we have a  lot more than most other countries do  oh it's so interesting talking to you  juliet davenport thank you  thank you lovely to talk to you well  that is all for today coming up on  tomorrow's show  the story of britain's acid tar lagoons  polluting local land with toxic liquid  you can see that at the same time here  on  sky news hope to see you then

